<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Awlad Mbarak emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1860 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Hassani Arab emirate of the Hodh in south-eastern Mauritania, a desert-edge power that raided and traded with the Bambara kingdoms of the Sahel.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Awlad Mbarak were a Hassani warrior group, part of the Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hassan">Bani Hassan</a> who had spread across the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. By the 17th century they dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodh">Hodh</a> region, in present-day south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is documented in detail. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> led warrior lineages that took tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> scholarly tribes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> herders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soninke">Soninke</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bambara</a> farmers. They traded salt, gum and livestock, and fought or allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kaarta">Kingdom of Kaarta</a> and other Sahelian states.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> declined in the 19th century under pressure from rival Hassani groups and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kaarta">Kaarta</a>. By around 1860 it had lost its power, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodh">Hodh</a> later passed under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-sudan">French Sudan</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-mauritania">French Mauritania</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1600 CE – 1860 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.54%;width:5.17%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1860 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hassaniya Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, <a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Awlad Mbarak, Emirate of the Hodh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1730&amp;focus=awlad-mbarak-emirate" aria-label="Awlad Mbarak emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4417 1452 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Awlad Mbarak emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4701 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4737 1708h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4737 1708h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Mauritania. <a href="/atlas?year=1730&amp;focus=awlad-mbarak-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

